  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Recurrent major depression without response to sertraline (F33.2)</content>. PHQ-9 22 to 17 after an adequate trial (five weeks at the maximum dose and twelve weeks in all): a 23 percent fall, short of a response. Sodium 137. Safety reviewed: no thoughts of suicide; medicines locked; 988 in his phone. Discussed with Dr. Lindqvist-Adebayo by phone: with no response rather than a partial one, switching to an SNRI is preferred over augmenting sertraline. ECT is not indicated now: there are no psychotic features, he is eating and drinking, and he is not acutely suicidal. [S]<list><item>Cross-taper: sertraline 100 mg for one week, then stop; venlafaxine extended-release 75 mg for one week, 150 mg for three weeks, then 225 mg.</item><item>Blood pressure at every visit on venlafaxine.</item><item>30-day supplies, because venlafaxine is more dangerous than an SSRI in overdose; medicines stay locked.</item><item>Call for agitation, sweating, tremor or fever during the switch.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Survey</td><td>PHQ-9</td><td>Baseline and at least monthly during acute treatment (CMID)</td><td>Completed today</td><td>17, down 23 percent from 22; item 9 scored 0</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>PHQ-9 between visits, rated by the psychologist at each session</td><td>At each therapy session</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Rated at each session since the last visit; no positive item 9 reported</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Suicide-risk review (C-SSRS screen since the last visit), safety plan, locked medicines and the 988 Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline</td><td>Every visit during active treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No thoughts of suicide; medicines locked; safety plan reviewed</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Sodium (SSRI or SNRI in an older adult)</td><td>Baseline, about four to six weeks after starting, and with symptoms</td><td>Completed today</td><td>137 mmol/L, normal</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Adequacy of the antidepressant trial (dose and duration)</td><td>At each review: at least four to eight weeks at an adequate dose before judging response</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Adequate trial: five weeks at 200 mg; no response; switch to venlafaxine</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Adherence and adverse-effect review</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Every dose taken; mild loose stools</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Switch safety: serotonin syndrome and discontinuation symptoms</td><td>During a cross-taper</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Warning signs explained; sertraline tapered over one week</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Blood pressure (venlafaxine)</td><td>Baseline and at every visit on venlafaxine</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Baseline 128/80 before venlafaxine</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Overdose risk and supply</td><td>With each prescription</td><td>Completed today</td><td>30-day supplies; locked box</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Watch for hypomania or mixed features after starting or changing an antidepressant</td><td>Every visit; repeat the MDQ if features emerge</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No signs of hypomania</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Behavioral activation with a psychologist</td><td>Weekly for about sixteen sessions</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Eleven sessions; activity schedule continuing</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>ECT indications</td><td>At each review of a severe episode</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>No psychosis, refusal of food or drink, or acute suicidal risk; revisit if a second trial fails</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Social work for income and costs</td><td>Monthly</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Monthly meetings continue</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Response to treatment: PHQ-9 down by at least half from 22. In progress; target 1957-10-05 [S]</item><item>Remission: PHQ-9 below 5. In progress; target 1957-10-05 [S]</item><item>Stay safe: safety plan in place and reviewed at every visit, with no self-harm. In progress; target 1957-10-05 [S]</item><item>Sing again: daily vocal practice and one public engagement a month. In progress; target 1957-10-05 [S]</item><item>Sleep six to seven hours a night. In progress; target 1957-10-05 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: APA Clinical Practice Guideline for the Treatment of Depression Across Three Age Cohorts (2019); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for the Management of Major Depressive Disorder (2022); The Practice of Electroconvulsive Therapy: Recommendations for Treatment, Training, and Privileging. A Task Force Report of the American Psychiatric Association, 2nd edition (record of the American Journal of Psychiatry review, 2002) (2001); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for Assessment and Management of Patients at Risk for Suicide (2024); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, depression, bipolar, and mood disorders monograph (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Nurse phone check at one week of the switch. Review at about eleven weeks on venlafaxine with the PHQ-9, sodium and blood pressure. Weekly behavioral activation. Call or text 988 at any time in a crisis. Next scheduled visit 1957-08-24: twenty-four-week review: partial response to venlafaxine; treatment-resistant depression; aripiprazole augmentation and an informed consent discussion about ECT. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
